WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.396
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:17.538
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Thinking Like a Historian. Today you will make careful, evidence-based historical claims with hedging.

4
00:00:17.838 --> 00:00:20.607
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.695
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:26.609
Source.

7
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:31.613
Source means a document or object that provides evidence about the past.

8
00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:34.692
Always identify who created a source.

9
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:38.040
Your turn. Say: Source.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:41.913
Source.

11
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.477
Evidence.

12
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:47.098
Evidence means information supporting a claim.

13
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:50.391
The evidence suggests that trade was a key cause.

14
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:53.415
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

15
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:57.314
Evidence.

16
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.144
Context.

17
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.891
Context means the circumstances around an event or source.

18
00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:06.982
Context explains why the law was passed.

19
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:09.934
Your turn. Say: Context.

20
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.952
Context.

21
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.101
Interpretation.

22
00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:20.428
Interpretation means an explanation of what evidence means.

23
00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:23.492
Her interpretation was widely accepted.

24
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.724
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

25
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:30.928
Interpretation.

26
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:32.580
Bias.

27
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:35.862
Bias means a one-sided perspective.

28
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.531
Every source contains some bias.

29
00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:41.421
Your turn. Say: Bias.

30
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:45.384
Bias.

31
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.267
Chronology.

32
00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:51.003
Chronology means the order in which events happened.

33
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:54.208
A clear chronology helps us see causes.

34
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:57.515
Your turn. Say: Chronology.

35
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.472
Chronology.

36
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:03.253
Causation.

37
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.072
Causation means the relationship between causes and effects.

38
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:11.159
Causation is rarely due to one factor.

39
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.263
Your turn. Say: Causation.

40
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.206
Causation.

41
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.114
Significance.

42
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:24.846
Significance means importance, especially in its effects.

43
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.123
Historians debate the significance of the treaty.

44
00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:31.497
Your turn. Say: Significance.

45
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:35.729
Significance.

46
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:40.114
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.088
Visal, this is our final unit. What does it mean to think like a historian?

48
00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:55.244
It means making careful claims. Instead of 'trade caused the kingdom's growth', I'd write 'the evidence suggests that trade was a key cause'.

49
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.966
Why hedge if you're confident?

50
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:09.760
Because sources are incomplete and biased. It appears likely that several factors interacted, so a single cause would be an oversimplification.

51
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.569
And how do historians decide what is significant?

52
00:03:14.683 --> 00:03:23.742
By asking how far an event changed people's lives, how long its effects lasted, and whether it still seems to shape the present.

53
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.516
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:30.603
Grammar focus: Hedging in historical claims.

55
00:03:30.903 --> 00:03:32.218
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.917 --> 00:03:35.775
The evidence suggests that trade was a key cause.

57
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.472
It appears likely that several factors interacted.

58
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:43.463
The sources seem to indicate that the decline was gradual.

59
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:55.539
Historians hedge to match the strength of the claim to the strength of the evidence: suggests, indicates, appears, seems, it is likely that, arguably. 'Evidence' is uncountable, so use 'suggests'.

60
00:03:56.283 --> 00:04:00.364
Be careful! Don't say: The evidence suggest that trade mattered.

61
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.194
Say: The evidence suggests that trade mattered.

62
00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:07.807
'Evidence' is singular: suggests.

63
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.159
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:13.606
The Historian's Habits.

65
00:04:13.906 --> 00:04:20.643
Throughout this course, we have seen that history is not a fixed list of facts but a disciplined argument about evidence.

66
00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:26.617
Thinking like a historian means asking who created a source, when, why and for whom.

67
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.431
It also means placing events in their context and chronology before judging their causes and significance.

68
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:41.344
Because evidence is always incomplete, careful historians hedge their claims, stating that the evidence suggests rather than proves.

69
00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:48.454
These habits are valuable far beyond history, helping citizens to evaluate the claims they meet every day.

70
00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:53.743
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:57.523
The evidence blank that the city was abandoned slowly.

72
00:04:57.823 --> 00:05:01.639
A, suggest. B, suggests. C, suggesting.

73
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.362
The answer is B: suggests.

74
00:05:08.517 --> 00:05:11.015
Choose the most appropriately hedged claim.

75
00:05:11.315 --> 00:05:19.160
A, Trade definitely caused everything.. B, It appears likely that trade was a major factor.. C, Trade is the only cause, obviously..

76
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.033
The answer is B: It appears likely that trade was a major factor..

77
00:05:28.183 --> 00:05:30.624
The order in which events happened is the blank.

78
00:05:30.924 --> 00:05:34.891
A, chronology. B, significance. C, context.

79
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:40.503
The answer is A: chronology.

80
00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:43.533
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:48.265
A, draw a conclusion. B, pull a conclusion. C, make up a conclusion.

82
00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.264
The answer is A: draw a conclusion.

83
00:05:55.417 --> 00:05:59.297
According to the reading, why do careful historians hedge their claims?

84
00:05:59.597 --> 00:06:06.327
A, because evidence is always incomplete. B, to sound uncertain. C, because sources are never biased.

85
00:06:09.867 --> 00:06:13.584
The answer is A: because evidence is always incomplete.

86
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.161
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.017 --> 00:06:21.585
How do historians decide what is significant?

88
00:06:38.383 --> 00:06:39.826
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.126 --> 00:07:18.626
Historians judge significance by how deeply an event affected people's lives and how long its consequences lasted. An event that changed the lives of many people, such as independence in 1953, is usually considered highly significant. Historians also ask whether an event reveals something important about the past or still shapes the present. However, significance can change over time, since each generation asks new questions. So the evidence suggests that significance is partly a matter of interpretation.

90
00:07:18.926 --> 00:07:20.542
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:26.030
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging in historical claims.

92
00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:31.188
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:07:31.488 --> 00:07:37.138
Congratulations! You have finished Online C one, Book three. See you in Online C two!

94
00:07:37.438 --> 00:07:40.555
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

